libva-v4l2 (1.0.0-4) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Track upstream's feat/encode-entrypoints branch: it adds the encode
    path, VAEntrypointEncSlice for H.264 (CBR/VBR/CQP) and HEVC Main on the
    encoder node the kernel exposes next to the decoder, so ffmpeg and
    GStreamer can encode in hardware. libva_src_ref now defaults to this
    branch instead of main.
  * That branch installs no iris-vaapi-browser launcher and no
    "Google Chrome (Iris VA-API)" desktop entry — libva resolves the driver
    through the msm_drv_video.so alias, so clients need neither. It installs
    iris-device-check.sh, iris-verify.sh, iris-configure-clients.sh and
    share/iris-vaapi/iris-node.sh instead, and the CI verification now
    asserts those files rather than main's launcher layout.
  * Recommends: ffmpeg — iris-verify exits 2 without ffmpeg/ffprobe.

 -- GhfunNiHe <131235947+GhfunNiHe@users.noreply.github.com>  Tue, 27 Oct 2026 19:45:00 +0800

libva-v4l2 (1.0.0-3) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Switch upstream to FFT-infra/qualcomm-iris-vaapi, tracking its main
    branch (the fork of phxinyang/qualcomm-iris-vaapi). The workflow input
    libva_src_ref now defaults to main instead of a hard-coded radxa commit,
    and libva-v4l2/COMMIT is gone; pass a tag or a commit there when a
    reproducible build is wanted. Same mxsrc/libva-v4l2
    ancestry as the radxa fork, but the stateful Iris implementation is
    rewritten: one ownership model with three publish policies chosen per
    surface from the client's export behaviour — Direct (the completed
    CAPTURE slot is handed over), Import (a buffer the client pre-exported is
    queued into CAPTURE, the zero-copy path Chrome takes) and Copy (GPU blit
    into a stable buffer, used for VP9 and on stock kernels).
  * Decode only: the backend exposes no VAEntrypointEncSlice, so the
    vah264enc/vah265enc GStreamer ranks are dropped from the shipped
    environment files and FastCV is gone with the -Dfastcv option.
  * msm_drv_video.so is now generated by debian/rules: the new meson.build
    installs v4l2_drv_video.so only, and libva probes the msm alias by DRM
    name, so a missing alias is a silent software fallback.
  * Depends: v4l-utils — the launcher locates the decoder node by the
    iris_driver QUERYCAP name at runtime instead of a /dev/videoN literal.
  * Ships the upstream iris-vaapi-browser launcher and its
    "Google Chrome (Iris VA-API)" desktop entry (installed by meson).
  * Still pairs with the iris decode-order patch in linux-redmi-marble, but
    no longer requires it: without the display-delay controls the driver
    degrades to GPU copy instead of aborting with "Invalid argument".

 -- GhfunNiHe <131235947+GhfunNiHe@users.noreply.github.com>  Thu, 22 Oct 2026 18:40:00 +0800

libva-v4l2 (1.0.0-2) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Package the radxa fork for marble unchanged: it is the only backend that
    speaks the Iris stateful V4L2 M2M interface (upstream mxsrc/libva-v4l2 is
    stateless-only), and Debian ships no Qualcomm VA driver at all, so
    browsers and ffmpeg otherwise have no hardware decode path.
  * Pairs with the iris decode-order patch in linux-redmi-marble: the backend
    sets V4L2_CID_MPEG_VIDEO_DEC_DISPLAY_DELAY{,_ENABLE} in its decoder
    constructor and aborts with "Invalid argument" when the kernel does not
    expose them. Verified on marble (SM7475): H.264, HEVC and VP9 1080p all
    decode through /dev/video0 with this pair installed.
  * Keeps the msm_drv_video.so alias, so vaInitialize() finds the driver with
    no LIBVA_DRIVER_NAME/LIBVA_DRIVERS_PATH set, and the GStreamer VA rank
    overrides in /usr/lib/environment.d + /etc/profile.d.

